  "terminal_reason": "completed",
  "final_text": "I created the dashboard and the alert rule, and the rule is enabled and has run once without errors. I haven't confirmed that its 5xx filter matches any documents. `KIBANA_TEST_API_KEY` is empty in the shell, so my direct test query was rejected as unauthorized, and the rule's \"ok\" status doesn't show whether anything matched.\n\n**1. What the data holds.** The `Kibana Sample Data Logs` data view uses `timestamp` as its time field. Each document is one web request, with:\n- the response code (`response`), bytes sent (`bytes`) and URL (`url`)\n- the client IP (`clientip`), source and destination country (`geo.src` / `geo.dest`) and coordinates\n- the browser (`agent`), operating system (`machine.os`), referrer, file extension and tags\n\n**2. Dashboard: \"EFF-5e9749a7 Web logs overview\"** (id `eff-5e9749a7-web-logs-overview-8d6bc99a41d5`). Link: http://localhost:15601/app/dashboards#/view/eff-5e9749a7-web-logs-overview-8d6bc99a41d5\n\nIt has 7 panels:\n- Total requests (number)\n- Unique visitors (count of distinct client IPs)\n- Requests over time (line chart)\n- Responses by status code (pie chart)\n- Bytes transferred over time (area chart)\n- Top 10 requested URLs (table)\n- Top 10 source countries (bar chart)\n\n**3. Alert rule: \"EFF-5e9749a7 5xx spike\"** (id `605c69ee-67fc-4716-a657-9447012f4cee`)\n- It's an Elasticsearch query rule on `kibana_sample_data_logs` that runs every minute.\n- It counts requests whose `response.keyword` starts with \"5\" (any 5xx code) in the last 5 minutes and fires when the count is above 10.\n- It has no actions attached, so when it fires the alert only shows up in Kibana and no notification is sent. To get notified you'd need to add a connector, such as Slack or email.\n\nTo check the filter, open the rule in Kibana's Rules page to see its match count, or run `response.keyword: 5*` in Discover.",
